            <ArticleTitle>Shuttle walking test in obese women: test-retest reliability and concurrent validity with peak oxygen uptake.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>120-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/cpf.12135</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim of this study was to evaluate the test-retest reliability, concurrent validity and agreement with peak oxygen uptake (VO2 ) obtained during cardiopulmonary exercise testing (CPX) on a treadmill for the incremental shuttle walking test (ISWT) in obese women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective study with a convenient sample of 46 community-dwelling obese women (BMI &gt; 30 kg m(-2) ). The main outcome measures were walking distance on the ISWT and peak VO2 .</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Test-retest reliability was good to excellent for the exercise tests (ISWT distance ICC: 0.90; and CPX peak VO2 ICC: 0.90). Peak VO2 obtained during CPX correlated significantly with ISWT distance (r = 0.54, P&lt;0.05) and peak VO2 obtained during the ISWT (r = 0.64, P&lt;0.05). Bland and Altman plots demonstrated a high degree of repeatability.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The ISWT had excellent reliability as well as good concurrent validity and agreement. The ISWT may be a potential tool for monitoring clinical status and intervention efficacy (e.g. programmes for weight loss and rehabilitative strategies) in this population.</AbstractText>
